# Copy to `.dev.vars` for local `wrangler dev`. In production set each as a
# secret: `wrangler secret put <NAME>`.

# One Ed25519 private JWK (a single line) signing every card this Worker serves
# and every push-notification callback JWT. MUST include a `kid`. Generate with
# `npm run keygen`.
#
# Only the matching PUBLIC key is ever exposed, at /.well-known/jwks.json — the
# private half never leaves the Worker.
#
# One key, not one per agent: the card lives at a well-known URI, which RFC 8615
# defines per-authority, so this origin serves one card and the gateway pins one
# key. The agents share this Worker and this file anyway, so per-agent keys never
# expressed a boundary that existed. What separates them is the tenant claim in
# the gateway's token, which core checks against the tenant each request names.
A2A_SIGNING_KEY=

# GATEWAY_ORIGINS: JSON array of trusted gateway origins, e.g.
# ["https://gateway.example.com"]. A property of this deployment, like the key.
# Not a traditional secret, but kept out of wrangler.jsonc so it can differ per
# environment with no code change.
GATEWAY_ORIGINS=

# ARC_API_KEY: API key for the ARC-AGI-3 game API (three.arcprize.org), issued
# from the ARC-AGI-3 web console. Required only by the arc-player agent
# (@loopingai/plugins/arc-agi declares it). Delete this line if you remove it.
ARC_API_KEY=

# There is NO Claude credential in this file, deliberately.
#
# The coder reaches Claude through an AI Gateway *custom provider* whose origin
# is the sibling `looping-anthropic-proxy` Worker, and that Worker is what holds
# the Anthropic tokens (ANTHROPIC_TOKEN_PRIMARY / _FALLBACK, both from
# `claude setup-token`). This deployment authenticates to it with a 120-second
# token it signs using A2A_SIGNING_KEY above — so nothing here is worth stealing
# for Claude access, rotating the Claude token never touches this Worker, and a
# coder container still never sees one.
#
# The route is a custom provider rather than the gateway's provider-native
# `/anthropic` path because that path is provider-aware: the gateway knows
# Anthropic's auth scheme and can supply the credential itself through BYOK or
# Unified Billing, so the token you send is not necessarily the one that ends up
# doing the work. A custom provider forwards `Authorization` untouched.
#
# What pairs this Worker with that one is the two origins below, and both must
# agree with the proxy's own or it refuses the request (surfacing as
# `proxy-credential`, not as a Claude problem):
#
#   SELF_ORIGIN               must appear in the proxy's CALLER_ORIGINS
#   ANTHROPIC_PROXY_AUDIENCE  must equal the proxy's PROXY_AUDIENCE

# SELF_ORIGIN: this deployment's own public origin, e.g.
# https://looping-starter.example.workers.dev — under `wrangler dev`, whatever
# origin you actually serve on.
#
# It is the `iss` of the token the coder mints for the proxy and the base of the
# `jku` pointing at the JWKS the proxy verifies against, so it must be the origin
# that really serves /.well-known/jwks.json.
#
# Not a traditional secret — an origin is public — but per-deployment, so it is
# kept out of wrangler.jsonc for the same reason GATEWAY_ORIGINS is: production,
# staging and a fork each need a different one, with no code change and no
# example value inherited by default.
SELF_ORIGIN=

# ANTHROPIC_PROXY_AUDIENCE: the proxy's public URL, used as the token's `aud`.
# Bounding the audience is what makes a captured token useless against any other
# service that trusts this same signing key. Per-deployment for the same reason
# as SELF_ORIGIN above.
ANTHROPIC_PROXY_AUDIENCE=

# AI_GATEWAY_TOKEN: authenticates this Worker to AI Gateway itself — a different
# authority from the Anthropic credentials, and a different token again from the
# proxy one described above. Three in total between a round and Claude.
#
# Needed because the coder is the only agent that calls a gateway URL directly;
# the other three reach it through the `AI` binding, which the platform
# authenticates. With Authenticated Gateway enabled (AI Gateway → your gateway →
# Settings), a request without this is rejected at the door: a 401 that Anthropic
# never sees and the gateway never logs, which looks exactly like an expired
# Claude token.
#
# Mint it at AI Gateway → your gateway → Settings → Create authentication token
# (it needs `AI Gateway Run`), then `npx wrangler secret put AI_GATEWAY_TOKEN`.
#
# Required only by the coder agent. Delete this line if you remove it, or leave
# it empty if your gateway has authentication turned off.
AI_GATEWAY_TOKEN=

# GITHUB_TOKEN: used by @loopingai/plugins/repo to clone, push a work branch and
# open the pull request. Needs `contents: write` and `pull-requests: write` on
# the repositories the coder is pointed at — a fine-grained PAT scoped to those
# repos is the right shape, not a classic token with org-wide access.
#
# It never enters the container as a command argument, it is offered only to the
# hosts on the repo plugin's allowlist (github.com by default) — a clone URL
# pointing anywhere else is refused before git runs — and pull requests are
# opened from the Worker rather than the sandbox, so the container never holds a
# credential that can write to the repository through the API.
#
# Required only by the coder agent. Delete this line if you remove it.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# There is deliberately nothing else here.
#
# `ENVIRONMENT` used to live at the bottom of this file, selecting between
# @cloudflare/sandbox's two backup paths (presigned URLs in production, a direct
# R2 binding under `wrangler dev`). The coder no longer snapshots its container
# to R2 at all — that path required R2 S3-API credentials a Workers binding
# cannot supply, and failed on every task in production — so the var had no
# reader and was removed along with it. See wrangler.jsonc.
